<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>A reusable reader.</h2>
  <p>BERT showed that reading with both sides of context could prepare one architecture for many language tasks. The same pretrained starting point could be adapted to classify sentences, label words, or locate answers.</p>
  <p>Next, we return to models that write forward. GPT-2 explored how much a larger next-word predictor could do through the text placed before its answer.</p>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/gpt-2/"><span>Next · 2019</span><strong>GPT-2 and zero-shot ↗</strong><span>Can a task be hidden inside a piece of text?</span></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Masking, architecture, results & limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>What gets predicted?</h3>
    <p>A token is a piece of text. BERT uses WordPiece tokens, which can split a word into smaller pieces. It selects 15% of token positions as prediction targets. Of these selected positions, 80% become <code>[MASK]</code>, 10% receive a random token, and 10% stay unchanged. The target is always the original token. This reduces reliance on a mask symbol that is absent during ordinary task use.</p>
    <p>For selected positions M and corrupted input x̃, the masked-token loss is <code>L = −Σᵢ∈M log P(xᵢ | x̃)</code>. Minimising it rewards the original tokens. The loss applies at selected positions; it does not define a left-to-right probability for a complete sentence. Multiple hidden positions are predicted separately from their shared corrupted input.</p>
    <h3>One input, a shared reader</h3>
    <p>The input combines token, position, and text-span embeddings—learned lists of numbers. <code>[CLS]</code> is a special leading token used for classification; <code>[SEP]</code> marks span boundaries. The two spans attend to each other inside one encoder. BERT Base has 12 layers, 768-dimensional states, 12 attention heads (separate learned reads), and 110 million parameters (adjustable numbers). BERT Large has 24 layers, 1,024-dimensional states, 16 heads, and 340 million parameters.</p>
    <p>Pretraining uses BooksCorpus and English Wikipedia. Its second loss classifies span pairs: half contain an actual continuation, half a randomly sampled span. This is next-sentence prediction, not generation of the next sentence. Fine-tuning updates all pretrained parameters and a task-specific output; different tasks use separate copies.</p>
    <h3>What the results showed</h3>
    <p>Table 1 reports BERT Large at 86.7% accuracy on the matched MultiNLI test set, versus 82.1% for the listed OpenAI GPT baseline. MultiNLI asks whether a second sentence is supported, contradicted, or left undecided by the first. These are reported system results with different training setups, rather than an isolated test of attention direction.</p>
    <p>BERT’s original objective trains a reader, not a left-to-right text generator. It still needs labelled examples for the paper’s task adaptation. Hidden text can be ambiguous; success on these benchmarks does not establish reliable reasoning or factual answers.</p>
    <h3>Two follow-ups</h3>
    <p>RoBERTa changes the training recipe: more data and training, larger batches, fresh masks, longer text sequences, and removal of next-sentence prediction. Its improvements show the importance of the recipe as a whole. XLNet instead varies which positions are predicted before others. The written sentence stays in order; the prediction order changes. A separate query representation identifies the target position without revealing its token.</p>
    <h3>How the toy works</h3>
    <p>The toy keeps sentences whose left and/or right phrase exactly matches the revealed clues, then counts the possible middle words. A bar is a word’s share of those matches. No parameters are trained. It illustrates combining context, omitting BERT’s learned representations, attention, tokenisation, and training.</p>
  </div>
</details>
